  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B043",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for optical lattice clocks at the magic wavelength"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B028",
    "B035"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged: B043 remains the most compelling final public proposal. The magic-wavelength lattice resolved the central light-shift obstacle to using many trapped atoms for clock spectroscopy, and the resulting clocks have become independently reproducible instruments of exceptional precision. This is a bounded physical and metrological breakthrough, already demonstrated in fundamental measurements and geodesy, rather than a case dependent on a future redefinition of the second. Katori and Ye remain a coherent compact attribution for the defining concept and decisive experimental development.",
    "case_against": "The required historical test remains serious. The citation must separate the magic-wavelength lattice-clock breakthrough from the longer atomic-clock lineage, and the archival record must establish that no further contributor is inseparable from the central invention and realization. Until that review is complete, the concise two-person attribution is a proposal rather than a settled historical conclusion.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "johansson-goran",
        "point": "Johansson argues that the strongest case is the magic-wavelength lattice as a controllable many-atom quantum system, not merely improved clock performance, while cautioning about the wider optical-clock lineage.",
        "response": "I agree with both parts. Framing the award around optical lattice clocks at the magic wavelength identifies the specific physical advance and avoids claiming credit for atomic-frequency metrology as a whole. The lineage question should be resolved by archival review, but it does not erase the unusually clear experimental maturity of the bounded achievement."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson prefers B016 because OCT is a mature, bounded physical imaging invention, and argues that its clinical uptake is evidence of a robust original optical method rather than a substitute for physics merit.",
        "response": "That is a fair and important account of B016, which remains a strong alternative in the broader shortlist. I nevertheless retain B043 because independent laboratories have established a new general regime of precision measurement with a more compact proposed attribution; its merit rests on demonstrated physical capability, not on projected applications."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
        "point": "Lindroth maintains that trapped-ion quantum control and simulation are already mature achievements, while explicitly excluding any claim that scalable fault-tolerant quantum computation has been achieved.",
        "response": "I accept that narrower formulation of B023 and regard it as scientifically strong. My preference remains B043 because its prize boundary is more sharply tied to a particular instrumental invention whose reproducibility and performance are already established, whereas the proper compact attribution across the trapped-ion experimental lineage remains less clear."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether archival review supports limiting B043 credit to Hidetoshi Katori and Jun Ye without omitting another indispensable contribution.",
      "Whether the final citation can delimit the magic-wavelength lattice-clock advance sufficiently clearly from earlier atomic-clock developments."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No conflict apparent from the supplied profile; I report no collaboration or institutional connection with the proposed laureates."
  }
}
